<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kang Youwei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kang Youwei was a Chinese political thinker and reformer of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, born in Nanhai County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, in 1858. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1895 he, his student Liang Qichao and over 600 examination candidates petitioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> against the Treaty of Shimonoseki, and in 1898 both took a leading part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> ended the reform by a coup and ordered his arrest and execution, Kang fled abroad and organised the Protect the Emperor Society. He remained an advocate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, launched a failed coup in 1917 and set out a cosmopolitan utopia in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a> Shu. He died in Qingdao in 1927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kang-youwei.jpg" alt="Kang Youwei" width="330" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elmer Chickering · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kang_Yu-wei_cph.3a45114.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kang's family had produced scholars for thirteen consecutive generations, and he was raised among Confucian values in a house filled with books. He began formal study of the Confucian classics at six, and after his father died when he was eleven his grandfather Kang Zanxiu took charge of his education. As a teenager he was dissatisfied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a> and its eight-legged essays, and he practised Buddhist meditation as a relaxation, which was unusual for a Chinese scholar. A visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> in 1879 shocked him with its prosperity and began his interest in Western thought. In 1883 he founded the Anti-Footbinding Society near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1890 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, where Liang Qichao became his student after Kang criticised the traditional scholarship Liang had pursued. Kang turned to the New Text school and argued in works of 1891 that the Old Text classics were forgeries and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> had been a reformer. After the defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1895 he and Liang Qichao led the petition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a>. In 1898, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a>, he persuaded the emperor to turn folk religion temples that did not perform state sacrifices into schools, and the confiscations were soon reversed.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Cixi</a>'s coup ended the reform and she ordered his arrest and execution, accusing him of trying to have her assassinated. Kang fled and organised the Protect the Emperor Society. After 1912 he opposed the republic, and in 1917 he launched a failed coup in which General Zhang Xun occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and declared Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> restored on 1 July. Kang distrusted Zhang, fled to the American legation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-qirui">Duan Qirui</a> occupied the city on 12 July. Kang died at his home in Qingdao in 1927, aged 69.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His best-known and most controversial work is the Datong Shu, The Book of Great Unity, which grew out of his lecture notes of 1884 and was not published in full until 1935. It projects a world united under one democratic government. It calls for the abolition of the family, with state nurseries and schools and one-year marriage contracts, and for equality of men and women. It also sets out a eugenics programme, described as inhumane, to eliminate darker phenotypes over a millennium. Kang saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> as an evil, and he believed that technology would let people work three or four hours a day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> admired the work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kang Youwei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many temples were destroyed in the Taiping and Boxer rebellions; in 1898 <b>Kang Youwei</b> persuaded the emperor to turn temples into schools, and after the revolution of 1911 most temples were put to other uses or destroyed.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Reformist thinkers such as Tan Sitong, <b>Kang Youwei</b> and Liang Qichao responded to what they saw as China's deep crisis, which included political division, insurrection, opium addiction and growing foreign conflicts.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>After another uprising in 1900 he had to compete for the support of overseas Chinese with the reformist leaders <b>Kang Youwei</b> and Liang Qichao, and he spent much of 1903 to 1905 visiting Chinese communities across the world.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/three-principles-of-the-people">Three Principles of the People · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
